      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\(\\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we need to express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of each other. Let's start by defining the coordinates:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nFirst, simplify \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - y \\]\n\nSince both expressions equal \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[ x - 1 = 1 - y \\]\n\nSimplify this equation:\n\n\\[ x - 1 = 1 - y \\]\n\\[ x + y = 2 \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a straight line. Therefore, the points \\(\\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
