      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the plotted points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nWe can simplify these expressions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, let's solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). From the expressions for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we have:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nBy adding the two equations, we get:\n\\[ x + y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) + \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = 2 \\]\n\nThis equation is always true, regardless of the value of \\(t\\). Therefore, the line \\(x + y = 2\\) is the curve on which all the points \\((x, y)\\) lie.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
